  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "IT-C01-ciao",
      "sequence": 10,
      "title": "ciao — \"hi,\" and the word that means \"I am your slave\"",
      "headword": "ciao",
      "romanization": "ciao",
      "gloss": "hi / bye (informal)",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "pen",
      "derivedModality": "pen",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "writing-block"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:7bd946e9a0911e43",
      "notice": {
        "modality": "pen",
        "needs": [],
        "waitUntilStopped": [],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your hands, so it is not a driving lesson. You can listen to all of it now and come back to the parts that need looking at once you have stopped."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The most famous \"hello\" in the world — and it hides an astonishing history."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ciao = CHOW: ci before a vowel is the ch of church; -ao is one gliding syllable."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ciao began as the Venetian greeting s-ciào (vostro) — \"[I am your] slave / servant,\" a humble \"at your service.\" That s-ciào is Venetian for schiavo (\"slave\") from Medieval Latin sclavus. The very same word is English slave — and, astonishingly, Slav: medieval slaves were so often Slavic captives that the ethnic name became the word for \"slave\" across Europe. So every casual ciao is a fossil of \"I am your servant.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ciao is informal, and works both coming and going (\"hi\" and \"bye\") — for friends, family, peers. To someone you'd show respect, or on a first meeting, you reach for a polite daytime greeting instead. You will build that greeting from two small pieces later in this chapter. Doubled, ciao ciao, it's a breezy \"bye-bye.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "writing",
          "title": "Writing — follow the greeting you can see",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Keep ciao visible. Point to ci, the two letters that begin the ch sound, then slide your finger through ao. Trace the whole word once from c to o, and say CHOW."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is not a spelling test. Your eye and hand are only following one short greeting whose sound and meaning are still on the page."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"ciao\" (CHOW)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"ciao\" (*CHOW*)]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "its buried meaning — \"I am your slave/servant\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: its buried meaning — \"I am your slave/servant\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the English cousins — slave, Slav",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the English cousins — *slave*, *Slav*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did ciao literally mean, and what two English words share its root? (\"I am your slave\"; slave and Slav, from Latin sclavus.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "IT-W01-ciao-guided-copy",
      "sequence": 15,
      "title": "ciao — copy one greeting with help",
      "headword": "ciao",
      "romanization": "ciao",
      "gloss": "copy the first greeting once with the model still visible",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "pen",
      "derivedModality": "pen",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "writing-type",
        "writing-block"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:aabf5233b92fb743",
      "notice": {
        "modality": "pen",
        "needs": [
          "a pen and something to write on"
        ],
        "waitUntilStopped": [],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your hands, so it is not a driving lesson. You will want a pen and something to write on. You can listen to all of it now and come back to the parts that need looking at once you have stopped."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Point to ciao and say it once: CHOW. The model stays on the page."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "writing",
          "title": "Writing — one guided copy",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Copy ciao once on the line below it. Write ci, pause, then add ao. Look back at the model whenever you want. Check that the i has its dot."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the word as you finish. The four letters record the sound you already know; there is nothing to guess and no second word today."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Compare your copy with the model. If one letter differs, repair only that letter. No hiding, no spelling from memory, and no free sentence yet."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "IT-C01-buono",
      "sequence": 20,
      "title": "buono / buon — \"good\"",
      "headword": "buono / buon",
      "romanization": "buono / buon",
      "gloss": "good (adjective)",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:1a009286963919e4",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One small word meaning \"good.\" Learn it alone first; later lessons will show how it helps build several greetings."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "buono = BWOH-no (the uo is a glide). Before a masculine noun it clips to buon (bwon)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "buono (\"good\") from Latin bonus becomes English bonus, bonanza, bonbon, and (via French) bounty. Spanish, another daughter of Latin, made bonus into bueno; Italian kept it closer, buono."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: it agrees with the noun",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "buono changes to match its noun's gender and number: buono (m.), buona (f.), buoni / buone (pl.) — Latin's legacy across the Romance family. Before a masculine noun it can drop the -o to buon. For now, just hear and say that small change; the next nouns will give it a job."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"buono\", then clipped \"buon\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"buono\", then clipped \"buon\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "English cousins — bonus, bonanza",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: English cousins — *bonus*, *bonanza*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What Latin word is buono from, and what short form can it take before a masculine noun? (bonus; buon, with the final -o dropped.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "IT-C01-il-la-lo",
      "sequence": 30,
      "title": "il / la / lo — \"the,\" and the gender it carries",
      "headword": "il / la / lo",
      "romanization": "il / la / lo",
      "gloss": "the (masculine / feminine)",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:e2fe477a454e308a",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before your first noun: the words for \"the.\" Italian splits \"the\" by gender — a habit inherited from Latin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "il and lo (masculine \"the\") and la (feminine) from Latin ille / illa (\"that one\"), the pointing word worn down to \"the.\" The same ille/illa gave Spanish el/la and French le/la. English \"the\" is Germanic and unrelated."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: gender, and two masculine \"the\"s",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Every Italian noun is masculine or feminine — Latin's three genders (the neuter folding into the masculine) collapsed to two. Learn each noun with its article. Italian has two masculine articles: lo before tricky clusters at the start of a noun, il in the ordinary pattern; feminine is always la. Your first noun comes next, so you can practise one real pairing then."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"il\", \"la\", \"lo\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"il\", \"la\", \"lo\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "their source — Latin ille / illa",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: their source — Latin *ille / illa*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What Latin word gave il / lo / la, and how many genders does Italian have? (ille / illa; two — masculine and feminine.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "IT-C01-giorno",
      "sequence": 40,
      "title": "giorno — \"day,\" and the road to English journey",
      "headword": "giorno",
      "romanization": "giorno",
      "gloss": "day (masculine)",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:41d5c0fe0be4d53c",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Your first noun — masculine, and a cousin of journal and journey."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "gi = the j of jam; giorno = JOR-no."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "il giorno (masc.) from Latin diurnum (\"daytime\"), from dies (\"day\"). That detour through diurnum is why giorno looks nothing like dies:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "via diurnum: journal, adjourn, sojourn, journey (a day's travel);"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "via dies: diurnal, diary."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Italian giorno and French jour are near-twins here; Spanish, taking dies directly, got the very different día."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: masculine, plural by vowel-change",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "giorno is masculine (il giorno). Plural i giorni — Italian marks the plural by changing the final vowel (-o becomes -i), not by adding -s."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"il giorno\" (masc.), \"i giorni\" (pl.)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"il giorno\" (masc.), \"i giorni\" (pl.)]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the cousins — journal, journey, diary",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the cousins — *journal*, *journey*, *diary*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What Latin word gave giorno, and what English words share it? (diurnum from dies; journal, journey, diurnal, diary.) How does Italian form the plural? (Changes the final vowel: -o becomes -i.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
