              "utterances": [
                "ciao means hi / bye (informal).",
                "buongiorno means good day.",
                "buonasera means good evening.",
                "buonanotte means good night.",
                "grazie means thank you."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Every piece from a Latin root:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ciao from sclavus (\"slave\"; English slave, Slav)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "buono from bonus."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "giorno from diurnum (English journal, journey)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "notte from noctem, via the -ct- becomes -tt- rule (notte, not noche/nuit)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "grazie from gratia (\"grace\"; English grace, gratitude)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And two Italian habits: adjectives agree with the noun's gender (buon giorno m. / buona sera f.), and the plural is made by changing the final vowel (giorno becomes giorni), not adding -s."
            }
          ]
